WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.940 --> 00:00:04.760
Hunting for Easter eggs in movies has become
a passion for large numbers of movie geeks

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:05.460
out there.

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:09.960
In fact, there's no shortage of YouTube channels
whose whole deal is all about pointing out

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:13.480
Easter eggs you might have missed in movies
or even just their trailers.

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:18.000
Movie sites and wikis abound with lists of
Easter eggs to be found in your favorite flicks.

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:23.460
And yet, even with all that, there are some
Easter eggs from classic movies that don't

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:26.340
appear on any site or YouTube video for some
reason.

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:30.820
In fact, once we point them out to you, you'll
wonder how you missed these amazing Easter

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:32.140
Eggs for so, so long.

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:36.180
Here's a whole basket of movie Easter eggs
that no one has ever thought to point out

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:37.760
— until now.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:40.140
"Here kid, have an egg."

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:44.080
Malls attract strange company

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:49.610
The 1995 follow-up to Kevin Smith's seminal
cult film Clerks, Mallrats boasts any number

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:54.500
of unforgettable scenes: like the one where
a bearded Stan Lee pops up to talk about Ben

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:55.500
Grimm's junk.

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:57.720
Or the one with the schooner in the Magic
Eye.

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:01.020
"Wow!  A sailboat!"

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:02.300
"Shut up."

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:06.770
Or the one where Michael Rooker​​, currently
beloved for his roles in The Walking Dead

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:10.340
and Guardians of the Galaxy, licks butt juice
off his hand.

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:15.200
But for true cinephiles who are always looking
to trump each other by uncovering the coolest

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:19.780
and most obscure Easter eggs in movies, one
scene in particular stands out.

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:25.359
Early in the film, lovable slacker Brodie
gets dumped by his girlfriend Rene to date

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:26.440
Ben Affleck's character.

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:29.740
Affleck then kidnaps Brodie and beats him
up.

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:34.920
But when Brodie's friends, including Jay and
Silent Bob, ask him what happened, he lies

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:38.460
and says the mall's Easter Bunny is the one
who busted his nose.

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:42.700
"All I said was the Easter Bunny at the Menlo
Park Mall was more convincing, and he just

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:44.400
jumped the railing and knocked me down."

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:49.569
Jay and Silent Bob decide to avenge their
pal Brodie in the way they know best: tactless

00:01:49.569 --> 00:01:50.540
violence.

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:53.820
But eagle-eyed viewers will catch an added
layer to this scene.

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:59.040
If you look in the background, you'll see
several plastic Easter Bunnies holding baskets.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:00.520
And what's in those baskets?

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:01.859
Yeah, boy.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:02.620
It's Easter eggs.

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:07.160
Bill and Ted's Bogus Holiday

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:13.760
Bill and Ted's Bogus Journey is the vastly
underappreciated 1991 sequel to 1989's Bill

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:18.750
and Ted's Excellent Adventure, a movie that
somehow managed to both kick off Keanu Reeves'

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:20.620
career and end Alex Winter's.

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:25.560
While the original film is about Bill and
Ted traveling through time to gather historical

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:30.720
figures in order to pass their history presentation,
Bogus Journey focuses on the two teens getting

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:35.969
offed by evil robot versions of themselves,
going to Hell, and teaming up with the Grim

00:02:35.969 --> 00:02:38.300
Reaper to save their fiancées.

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:39.740
It rules.

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:45.140
"(awesome guitar lick)"

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:47.640
While in Hell, the two dudes are forced to

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:53.239
face their greatest fears, which include Bill's
grody grandmother and a military school colonel

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:55.000
who assigns them infinite push-ups.

00:02:55.460 --> 00:03:00.129
The scariest vision of all, however, is one
in which Ted is reverted to a younger version

00:03:00.129 --> 00:03:04.460
of himself who had stolen a basket full of
candy with his younger brother's name on it.

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:09.600
As he digs into the basket, he's confronted
by a Chucky-faced pink rabbit who chases and

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:11.800
taunts him for making his brother cry.

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:17.460
Yup — the basket Ted stole from his brother
was an Easter basket.

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:22.480
In other words: you guessed it, it was totally
loaded with delicious Easter eggs.

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:24.800
Guardians of the Gal-eggs-y

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:30.260
2012's Rise of the Guardians is an incredibly
rad animated film about the Guardians of Childhood,

00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:35.120
a secret society of magical figures who help
protect children and fight to keep childhood

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:36.100
magical for them.

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:42.700
Members of the team include Santa Claus, the
Tooth Fairy, the Sandman, Jack Frost, and

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:46.160
a mysterious fellow named E. Aster Bunnymund.

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:52.239
To make a long story short, it turns out that
this name is actually a cleverly hidden reference

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:56.620
to the Easter Bunny — which would totally
explain some of Bunnymund's characteristics.

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:01.160
Like, y'know, the fact that he is a large
talking bunny obsessed with Easter.

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:06.439
It also gives a new significance to the swarms
of seemingly sentient, mobile eggs that follow

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:07.040
him around.

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:12.380
Once you know Bunnymund is actually the Easter
Bunny, you might start to realize that that

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:16.360
these aren't the normal, everyday walking
colored eggs that you see all the time.

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:18.960
These are actually Easter eggs.

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:20.240
Cool, right?

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:25.760
And all it took to figure it out was a little
word puzzle solving.

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:29.740
Definitely makes all that time spent doing
word searches feel like it was worth it.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:33.360
"If you're not a kangaroo, what are you?"

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:37.200
"I'm a bunny — the Easter bunny."

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:39.400
Nightmare Before Easter

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:43.760
This next scene only makes sense thanks to
fan theories from the internet — but if

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:47.740
those theories are right, it makes for a cool
and unexpected Easter egg.

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:53.600
The Nightmare Before Christmas is a 1993 stop-motion
animated movie where every major holiday has

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:57.340
its own town devoted to the celebration of
that holiday.

00:04:57.620 --> 00:05:03.020
Jack Skellington, the Pumpkin King of Halloween
Town, accidentally stumbles across a portal

00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:05.540
to Christmas Town and becomes obsessed with
it.

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:10.870
He decides Halloween Town will run Christmas
this year, and so he sends his minions Lock,

00:05:10.870 --> 00:05:13.140
Shock, and Barrel to kidnap Santa Claus.

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:14.100
Why?

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:19.000
So Jack can do up the Yuletide in his own
stripey, spiraly, Tim Burton Hot Topic fever

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:20.370
dream aesthetic that year.

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:24.950
The trick-or-treating trio don't initially
come back with Santa Claus, however.

00:05:24.950 --> 00:05:30.930
Instead they bring back a roughly human-sized
rabbit carrying a basket and wearing a sash

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:33.020
that says "Happy Easter" on it.

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:39.180
Now here's where that cool fan theory kicks
in: Since each of the portals leads to a different

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:44.560
holiday, one of those doors might just lead
to some kind of Easter Town.

00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:47.060
We know — get out those crime boards, people!

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:53.660
That would mean that this man-sized, basket-toting
rabbit is actually the Easter Bunny, and those

00:05:53.660 --> 00:05:58.640
lumps in his basket are probably those tasty
Easter eggs we've been hunting!

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:00.820
That is, if the fan theory is true...

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:02.560
The big picture

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:07.530
Universal Pictures has been using an image
of the planet Earth with some version of its

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:11.660
name superimposed on it since basically the
beginning of its existence, even if it has

00:06:11.660 --> 00:06:14.240
evolved quite a bit in the studio's century-plus
history.

00:06:14.440 --> 00:06:19.320
One thing has been consistent, however: It's
always the logo on top of our regular, old,

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:21.550
round globe Earth.

00:06:21.550 --> 00:06:22.050
Or is it?

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:31.900
Hop is a 2011 film from Universal that finally
makes a movie out of the old traditional story

00:06:31.900 --> 00:06:36.430
we've all known and loved since childhood
–– that the Easter Bunny has a son who

00:06:36.430 --> 00:06:40.760
would rather play drums in a rock and roll
music band than deliver Easter baskets, and

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:43.440
whose dooks are actually jelly beans.

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:48.500
The genius behind the hidden Easter egg in
Hop is that it comes before the movie even

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:49.000
starts.

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:51.380
Take a gander at the flick's first few seconds.

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:55.080
That familiar Universal globe isn't quite
how you remember it, is it?

00:06:55.780 --> 00:06:56.880
It isn't round.

00:06:57.200 --> 00:06:59.380
And, no, it isn't flat either.

00:06:59.480 --> 00:07:01.580
It's kind of ... oval?

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:05.380
You might even call it...egg-shaped.

00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:07.860
The Easter Bunny Is Comin' to Town

00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:12.580
These days, Rankin/Bass Productions is perhaps
best known for its Christmas specials like

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:17.080
Rudolph the Red-Nosed Reindeer, Frosty the
Snowman, and Santa Claus Is Comin' to Town.

00:07:17.300 --> 00:07:22.340
But they actually produced a large number
of non-Christmas specials between 1964 and

00:07:22.340 --> 00:07:27.600
1987, including a series of three separate
origin films for the Easter Bunny in a span

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:28.400
of six years.

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:34.300
The last of these three Easter Bunny specials
was 1977's The Easter Bunny Is Comin' to Town.

00:07:34.460 --> 00:07:40.020
As the film's name implies, it's a sequel
to 1970's Santa Claus Is Comin' to Town, and

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:44.560
it actually follows the template set out by
that previous film maybe a little too closely.

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:50.300
Filling the role of the Winter Warlock from
Santa Claus is a grumpy bear named Gadzooks

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:55.120
who has a tendency to steal anything holiday-related
that anyone tries to carry past his home on

00:07:55.120 --> 00:07:55.920
Big Rock Mountain.

00:07:56.470 --> 00:08:01.020
In one scene, the future Easter Bunny, named
Sunny... just like James Caan's character

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:05.180
in The Godfather... tries to cross the mountain
only to be accosted by Gadzooks.

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:10.660
When Gadzooks inspects Sunny's basket looking
for snacks, he finds the basket is full of

00:08:10.660 --> 00:08:14.460
colored stones that Sunny intends to sell
as paperweights.

00:08:14.600 --> 00:08:19.250
A close viewing of the film, however, will
call to mind an earlier scene in which Sunny

00:08:19.250 --> 00:08:23.550
and his friends actually painted a large number
of eggs bright colors in order to specifically

00:08:23.550 --> 00:08:24.780
fool Gadzooks.

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:30.540
So not only does this scene feature some cool
Easter eggs, it actually reveals the true,

00:08:30.540 --> 00:08:34.800
totally, 100 percent, historically accurate
origin of Easter eggs.

00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:35.920
Not too shabby!

00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:37.920
Who Hard-Boiled Roger Rabbit

00:08:38.380 --> 00:08:43.800
1988's Who Framed Roger Rabbit is the most
ambitious crossover event in history, no matter

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:45.100
what Marvel Studios says.

00:08:45.380 --> 00:08:49.840
Where else can you see Mickey Mouse, Bugs
Bunny, Woody Woodpecker, Droopy, and Pinocchio

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:50.760
in one movie?

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:55.780
Anyway, one scene in particular stands out
to fans scouring this reference-laden flick

00:08:55.780 --> 00:08:56.400
for Easter eggs.

00:08:56.580 --> 00:09:01.640
In this scene, human detective Eddie Valiant
is taunted by an egg-peeling barfly for working

00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:02.960
a job with a toon.

00:09:03.260 --> 00:09:07.620
Eddie angrily shoves the egg in the lout's
mouth before we learn that Eddie's long-standing

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:10.440
anti-toon prejudice comes from the fact that
…

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:13.360
"A toon killed his brother.

00:09:14.420 --> 00:09:19.540
It might seem like a stretch, but could this
regular egg actually be an Easter egg?

00:09:19.880 --> 00:09:25.460
Hear us out: Disney is known for its perfectionism,
and its animated films are filled with Easter

00:09:25.460 --> 00:09:25.960
eggs.

00:09:26.200 --> 00:09:29.940
So, it's unlikely that such a noticeable choice
would be meaningless.

00:09:30.220 --> 00:09:34.620
So, a prominent inclusion of an egg in a movie
that stars a rabbit should be setting off

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:35.880
some alarm bells in your head.

00:09:36.040 --> 00:09:37.580
Egg plus rabbit?

00:09:37.980 --> 00:09:42.440
Sounds like Disney thought they could slip
an Easter egg past us without us noticing.

00:09:42.820 --> 00:09:43.940
Did we forget something?

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:44.640
Oh, right!

00:09:44.940 --> 00:09:48.680
Happy Easter 2018, and happy April Fools'
Day, too.

00:09:49.140 --> 00:09:50.140
Thanks for watching!

00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:52.720
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:09:52.720 --> 00:09:54.800
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

